751 0 obj
<</Title(The hot-stream inlet temperature was maintained at Th=1.0, while the cold-stream inlet temperature was maintained at Tc=0. For parallel flow with a wall-to-fluid ratio of 10 and Reynolds numbers of 300 for both hot and cold streams, the hot outlet temperature remained approximately 0.97, while the cold outlet temperature increased slightly from its inlet value. When the wall-to-fluid ratio was increased to 50 and the Reynolds numbers of both streams were reduced to 50, the hot outlet temperature decreased to approximately 0.94, and the cold outlet temperature increased to approximately 0.06. The temperature contours showed heat transfer from the hot stream through the conducting wall to the cold stream. The temperature residual decreased with increasing iterations, reaching approximately 10^-8, indicating convergence toward a steady-state solution. Overall, the results demonstrate the influence of flow conditions and wall thermal properties on the conjugate heat transfer performance of a double-pipe heat exchanger.)/Dest[420 0 R/Fit]/Parent 750 0 R>>
endobj

