1024 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(The internal fluid flow and heat transfer in a centrifugal pump were studied using a two-dimensional annular rotating-frame model for different pump speeds and Reynolds numbers. The inlet temperature was maintained at 300 K, while the wall temperature was set to 330 K. The thermal convergence plots showed a gradual decrease in the thermal residual, indicating a reduction in the numerical error during the iterative solution. The outlet temperature increased slightly from the inlet temperature, demonstrating heat transfer from the heated wall to the fluid. The pressure contours illustrated the radial pressure distribution within the pump passage, with higher pressure observed toward the outer radius. Increasing the pump speed is expected to increase the centrifugal pressure rise, approximately following the square of rotational speed under comparable conditions.)/E(The internal fluid flow and heat transfer in a centrifugal pump were studied using a two-dimensional annular rotating-frame model for different pump speeds and Reynolds numbers. The inlet temperature was maintained at 300 K, while the wall temperature was set to 330 K. The thermal convergence plots showed a gradual decrease in the thermal residual, indicating a reduction in the numerical error during the iterative solution. The outlet temperature increased slightly from the inlet temperature, demonstrating heat transfer from the heated wall to the fluid. The pressure contours illustrated the radial pressure distribution within the pump passage, with higher pressure observed toward the outer radius. Increasing the pump speed is expected to increase the centrifugal pressure rise, approximately following the square of rotational speed under comparable conditions.)/K[19]/P 980 0 R/Pg 756 0 R>>
endobj

