1261 0 obj
<</Title(The flow around a circular cylinder was numerically investigated using computational fluid dynamics at a Reynolds number of 5000, with a computational grid of 350 \327 350 and 2000 outer time steps. The lift coefficient initially exhibited small oscillations, followed by a significant increase in oscillation amplitude toward the end of the simulation, indicating the development of unsteady flow and vortex shedding. The drag coefficient showed a sharp initial transient before settling at ~1.3, with minor fluctuations at later. The vorticity contour reveals that alternating regions of positive and negative vorticity downstream of the cylinder, forming a characteristic vortex street. These observations demonstrate the influence of inertial effects on wake formation and aerodynamic forces at a higher Reynolds number. However, the increasing amplitude of the lift fluctuations suggests that a stable periodic state may not have been fully established within the simulated duration. Further simulation with sufficient time steps and verification of numerical stability would help obtain more reliable results.)/Dest[1058 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1260 0 R>>
endobj

