1699 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(The two-dimensional steady-state heat conduction in a flat plate was analyzed by solving Laplace\220s equation using the Jacobi iterative method and the line-wise TDMA method. The temperature profiles obtained using both numerical methods closely matched the analytical solution, demonstrating their ability to predict the temperature distribution in the plate. The temperature contours showed that the temperature was highest near the heated left boundary and gradually decreased toward the right boundary, where the temperature was maintained at zero. The convergence plots indicated that Line TDMA reduced the residual much faster than the Jacobi method, requiring fewer iterations to achieve convergence. This difference arises because Jacobi updates the temperature point by point, whereas Line TDMA solves along grid lines. )/E(The two-dimensional steady-state heat conduction in a flat plate was analyzed by solving Laplace\220s equation using the Jacobi iterative method and the line-wise TDMA method. The temperature profiles obtained using both numerical methods closely matched the analytical solution, demonstrating their ability to predict the temperature distribution in the plate. The temperature contours showed that the temperature was highest near the heated left boundary and gradually decreased toward the right boundary, where the temperature was maintained at zero. The convergence plots indicated that Line TDMA reduced the residual much faster than the Jacobi method, requiring fewer iterations to achieve convergence. This difference arises because Jacobi updates the temperature point by point, whereas Line TDMA solves along grid lines. )/K[1]/P 1698 0 R/Pg 1480 0 R>>
endobj

